Here is the commit message:
Fix org.getMembers() to 'wrap' returned users for
 post-populating

This fixes an issue in which users returned by `getMembers()` were not
populated with a full set of values.

This commit just applies 'wrapping' to all users returned, so that methods
like `user.getCreatedAt()` can be called on returned users without throwing
a NullPointerException - the reference to 'root' allows the user objects to
populate themselves.

Here is the root cause:
In `GHOrganization`'s members-listing logic, the `PagedIterator<GHUser>` anonymous class overrode the `wrapUp` callback (which is invoked after each page of results is fetched) with an empty method body. This meant that the `GHUser` objects returned from the GitHub API for organization members were never associated with the `root` `GitHub` connection object. Since many `GHUser` fields (e.g., `createdAt`) are lazily populated on-demand via calls back through `root`, any user object lacking this `root` reference would throw a `NullPointerException` when such lazy-loaded getters were invoked. The fix ensures `GHUser.wrap(users, root)` is called to properly initialize each returned user with the necessary `root` reference, consistent with other similar listing methods in the codebase.

Here is the patch:

     private PagedIterable<GHUser> getMembers(final String suffix, final String filter) throws IOException {
         return new PagedIterable<GHUser>() {
             public PagedIterator<GHUser> iterator() {
                 String filterParams = (filter == null) ? "" : ("?filter=" + filter);
                 return new PagedIterator<GHUser>(root.retrieve().asIterator(String.format("/orgs/%s/%s%s", login, suffix, filterParams), GHUser[].class)) {
                     @Override
-                    protected void wrapUp(GHUser[] page) {
+                    protected void wrapUp(GHUser[] users) {
+                        GHUser.wrap(users, root);
                     }
                 };
             }
         };
     }



